From: Leszek Swirski <leszeks@chromium.org>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 16:58:35 +0200
Subject: [M154] [turbofan] Fix UseCount bailout in
 DuplicateStateValuesAndRename

Original change's description:
> [turbofan] Fix UseCount bailout in DuplicateStateValuesAndRename
>
> In JSInliningHeuristic::TryReuseDispatch, when duplicating and
> specializing frame states for inlined polymorphic call branches,
> DuplicateStateValuesAndRename recursively traversed StateValues trees.
> However, it bailed out early if state_values->UseCount() > 1.
>
> When a StateValues node has more than 8 inputs, it is split into a tree
> of nested StateValues nodes. When renaming in an earlier child subtree,
> the parent StateValues is cloned via CloneNode, which immediately
> increments the UseCount of all sibling child subtrees from 1 to 2.
> Subsequent recursive calls encountered UseCount() > 1 and bailed out
> without renaming the callee Phi in the sibling subtrees. When the callee
> Phi was later killed, the cloned FrameState retained dangling references
> to the dead node, causing verifier check failures.
>
> This CL removes the premature UseCount() > 1 early return from
> DuplicateStateValuesAndRename and ensures that kChangeInPlace only
> mutates when UseCount() <= 1, falling back to cloning (copy-on-write)
> otherwise.
>
> Note that removing this early return does not cause unnecessary copying
> for StateValues subtrees that already had UseCount() > 1 initially (e.g.
> shared with other frame states): if such a subtree contained the callee,
> CollectStateValuesOwnedUses would have skipped it, causing subsequent
> validation in TryReuseDispatch to abort before cloning began. Therefore,
> any subtree with UseCount() > 1 initially is guaranteed not to contain
> the callee, so DuplicateStateValuesAndRename visits its inputs, finds no
> matches, and returns the original node without copying.
>
> Fixed: 558734727
